---
import Tool from '../../layouts/Tool.astro'
import Calculator from '../../components/Calculator.astro'
import type { FaqEntry } from '../../types.ts'

const faq: FaqEntry[] = [
  {
    q: 'How accurate is a period calculator?',
    a: "It's only as accurate as the average cycle length you enter, and it assumes every future cycle will match that average exactly. Real cycles vary by a few days even in people who consider themselves regular, and small differences compound the further out a prediction reaches — treat the dates as a planning window, not a guarantee.",
  },
  {
    q: "What should I enter if I don't know my average cycle length?",
    a: 'Count from the first day of one period to the first day of the next for your last two or three cycles, then average the results. If you have never tracked this before, 28 days is a common starting point, but logging your own dates for a cycle or two will give you a far more personal number than any default.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why did I get an error message instead of a result?',
    a: "The calculator only accepts cycle lengths between 21 and 45 days and period lengths between 1 and 10 days — the outer edges of what's generally considered a physiologically plausible range. If your own numbers sit near those edges, or you had to guess wildly to get a value inside them, that pattern is worth raising with a doctor rather than working around.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use this to know which days are safe to avoid pregnancy?',
    a: "No. This calculator, and the app behind it, is not a method of contraception. It estimates likely dates from a fixed average; it cannot account for the cycle-to-cycle variation that makes calendar-based methods unreliable for preventing pregnancy on their own.",
  },
  {
    q: "My actual period didn't match the estimate. Is something wrong?",
    a: 'Not necessarily. The calculator assumes every future cycle is exactly the length you typed in, but real cycles fluctuate even in people with a consistent pattern, and the gap between the estimate and reality tends to grow the further into the future you look — a normal limitation of projecting forward from one fixed number rather than a sign anything is wrong.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this still work if my periods are already irregular?',
    a: "It will still produce a result, but treat it as a much rougher guide. The maths assumes one fixed cycle length repeating indefinitely; if your cycles vary a lot from month to month that assumption doesn't hold well, and a doctor can help work out why they're inconsistent in the first place.",
  },
  {
    q: 'How is this different from what the LunaTrack app does?',
    a: 'This page runs the same underlying calendar maths as the app, applied once to whatever last period date and cycle length you type in by hand. The app builds the same kind of estimate from your own logged cycle history instead of a single manual entry, so it can adjust as your actual pattern changes across cycles rather than staying fixed to one guess.',
  },
]
---
<Tool
  title="Period Calculator — Predict Your Next Period"
  description="Estimate your next three period dates from your last start date and cycle length, calculated in your browser and never transmitted anywhere."
  path="/tools/period-calculator"
  faq={faq}
>
  <Calculator slot="widget" kind="period" />

  <p>
    A period calculator answers one narrow question: if your next few cycles
    behave like your recent ones, when will your period probably start and
    end? Type in the first day of your last period, how long your cycle
    usually runs, and how many days you typically bleed, and this tool
    projects your next three periods forward using the same arithmetic a
    calendar and a pen would use — just faster, and less error-prone. It runs
    entirely as JavaScript in your own browser; the dates you type are never
    sent to LunaTrack or to anyone else, and nothing here requires the app
    itself. If you'd rather see how this fits alongside the rest of what
    LunaTrack tracks, the <a href="/tools" class="underline">calculators
    page</a> lists the other estimators built the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>How this works</h2>
  <p>
    The calculation is deliberately simple, and that simplicity is the whole
    point: it makes the assumptions visible instead of hiding them inside a
    black box. You give the tool three numbers — a start date, a cycle
    length, and a period length — and it does the same thing you'd do with a
    paper calendar: starting from your last period's first day, it counts
    forward one full cycle length to land on the next expected start date,
    then counts forward again by the same amount for the cycle after that,
    and once more for the one after that. For each predicted start date, it
    adds your period length minus one day to mark where that period is
    expected to end.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no hidden adjustment, no smoothing, and no attempt to guess
    whether this particular cycle will run long or short — the same cycle
    length is applied uniformly to every future date. That's a deliberate
    trade-off. A more elaborate model could try to weight recent cycles more
    heavily, or flag a cycle as an outlier, but doing that well needs a
    real history of your own cycles to learn from, not three numbers typed in
    once. This page works from what you hand it in the moment; the "Tracking
    instead of guessing" section below covers what changes once there's a
    real history behind the numbers. Because everything happens locally in
    your browser's own JavaScript engine, closing the tab discards the
    calculation completely — there's no record kept anywhere of what you
    typed in or what came out.
  </p>

  <h2>What "cycle length" means and how to measure it</h2>
  <p>
    Cycle length is the number of days from the first day of one period to
    the first day of the next — not the number of days in a month, and not
    the number of days between the end of one period and the start of the
    next. Day one of a cycle is the first day of actual bleeding, not
    spotting beforehand and not the day symptoms start; light spotting a day
    or two before flow becomes steady is common and doesn't count as day
    one for most people, though when it's genuinely hard to tell where
    bleeding starts, that ambiguity is itself worth mentioning to a doctor.
  </p>
  <p>
    To measure your own cycle length by hand: write down the date your last
    period started, then write down the date the next one starts, and
    subtract. A single measurement tells you one data point, not your
    "true" cycle length — length can shift by a few days from one cycle to
    the next even in people whose cycles are considered regular, so three or
    four consecutive measurements give a far more honest picture than one.
    If you've logged several start dates already, averaging them (or using a
    tool built for exactly that, like the cycle-length calculator this page
    links to once it's live) is more reliable than picking one recent
    example and assuming it's typical.
  </p>
  <p>
    A related point worth being precise about: this calculator restricts
    cycle length to between 21 and 45 days. That range isn't a claim about
    what's "normal" — reproductive-age cycles most often fall somewhere
    between about 24 and 38 days, and plenty of healthy cycles sit outside
    that narrower band too — it's simply the outer edge of what the
    underlying maths treats as a plausible input worth projecting forward.
    Numbers outside that range are rejected outright rather than silently
    accepted and used to produce a confident-looking but meaningless date.
  </p>

  <h2>Why your dates will drift</h2>
  <p>
    Every prediction this calculator makes rests on one assumption: that
    your next cycle, and the one after that, and the one after that, will
    each last exactly as long as the number you typed in. In practice, almost
    nobody's cycles are that metronomic. The first half of a cycle — from the
    start of a period to ovulation — is the part most prone to shifting from
    month to month, because ovulation timing itself responds to everything
    from sleep to illness to stress. The second half, from ovulation to the
    next period, tends to be far steadier, typically landing somewhere close
    to two weeks, which is part of why a due-date or ovulation estimate
    often counts backward from an expected period rather than forward from
    the last one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because the first half is the variable part, even a cycle that runs a
    few days long or short changes when the whole cycle finishes — and once
    that happens, every later prediction this tool makes is built on the
    now-outdated assumption that your cycle still matches the number you
    entered at the start. A three-day shift in one cycle doesn't just move
    that one period; it shifts every subsequent projected date by the same
    three days, because the calculator has no way to learn that your actual
    pattern changed unless you tell it again with a fresh calculation using
    your latest start date.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the single biggest limitation of any one-off calculator compared
    with ongoing tracking: a calculator answers the question "what if every
    future cycle matches this one number," while your actual cycles answer a
    messier, more honest question that changes slightly every month. Neither
    answer is wrong, but they're different questions, and it's worth knowing
    which one you're looking at when the two disagree.
  </p>

  <h2>What changes a cycle (illness, stress, travel, contraception, perimenopause)</h2>
  <p>
    Cycle length isn't fixed biology in the way height is — it responds to
    what's happening in the rest of the body, sometimes within a single
    cycle. A short illness with a fever can delay ovulation by disrupting
    the hormonal signalling between the brain and the ovaries, which pushes
    the whole cycle later even though the delay itself only lasted a few
    days. Psychological or physical stress works through a related pathway:
    sustained stress can suppress the hormone signals that trigger ovulation,
    sometimes delaying a period by days and occasionally skipping it
    altogether for that cycle.
  </p>
  <p>
    Travel across time zones, and disrupted sleep more generally, can shift
    cycle timing too — the reproductive hormone cycle is loosely coupled to
    the body's broader circadian rhythms, and jet lag or a run of bad sleep
    is a common, usually temporary, cause of a cycle running longer or
    shorter than expected. None of these on their own are cause for alarm;
    they're the reason cycle length is a range rather than a fixed number
    even for people whose cycles are usually predictable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hormonal contraception changes the picture more fundamentally. Combined
    pills, the patch, and the ring generally replace the natural cycle with
    a hormone level the method controls directly, which is why bleeding on
    these methods can be very regular but isn't really a "cycle" being
    predicted in the same sense — it's a scheduled withdrawal bleed. Coming
    off hormonal contraception, or switching methods, commonly causes a
    stretch of irregularity while the body's own signalling resumes, and
    that stretch can last several cycles before settling into a new pattern.
    A calculator like this one, built around a natural ovulatory cycle,
    won't model a hormonal method's bleeding pattern well, and isn't meant
    to.
  </p>
  <p>
    Perimenopause is the other major source of change, and it's one of the
    most common reasons cycle length shifts later in life. As ovarian
    reserve declines, cycles frequently become more variable — sometimes
    shorter for a stretch, sometimes longer, sometimes skipped — well before
    periods stop altogether. That variability is itself the pattern doctors
    look for, so if your cycles have become noticeably less predictable in
    your 40s or later, that's useful information to bring to a doctor rather
    than something a calculator can characterise for you.
  </p>

  <h2>When a late period is worth a doctor's visit</h2>
  <p>
    A period a few days later than expected is common and usually not a sign
    of anything wrong — it's exactly the kind of variation the section above
    describes. A few situations are worth treating differently. If pregnancy
    is possible, a late period is the first thing a pregnancy test can rule
    in or out, and that's a faster, more direct answer than any calculator
    can give. Missing periods for three months or longer, when they aren't
    explained by pregnancy, breastfeeding, or a contraceptive method that's
    expected to stop them, is generally considered worth discussing with a
    doctor — this pattern has a name, secondary amenorrhoea, and it has
    identifiable causes ranging from thyroid conditions to polycystic ovary
    syndrome to significant changes in weight or exercise.
  </p>
  <p>
    A sudden, sustained change in a pattern that used to be predictable is
    also worth a conversation, even if any single late period wouldn't be.
    So is bleeding that's unusually heavy — soaking through protection every
    hour or two for several hours, or passing clots larger than a coin —
    bleeding between periods or after sex, or pain severe enough to
    interfere with daily activities. None of these are things this
    calculator, or any calculator, can evaluate: it only knows the numbers
    you typed in, not what your body is actually doing. Its job stops at
    arithmetic; a doctor's doesn't.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of this is a diagnosis, and this page isn't trying to be one — it's
    a rough guide to which kinds of lateness are ordinary variation and
    which are worth a second opinion, so that a delayed period doesn't sit
    unexamined for months out of uncertainty about whether it's worth
    mentioning.
  </p>

  <h2>Tracking instead of guessing</h2>
  <p>
    Everything above points at the same conclusion: a single calculation
    from a single guessed cycle length is a reasonable starting point, but
    it's a snapshot, not a system. The more your actual cycle varies, the
    less a fixed-length projection has to say about any specific future
    date, and the more value there is in recording what actually happens
    each cycle instead of re-guessing the same average every time.
  </p>
  <p>
    That's the difference between a calculator and ongoing tracking. A
    calculator takes three numbers once and projects them forward blindly.
    Tracking takes the date your period actually starts, cycle after cycle,
    and uses that real history — not a remembered average — to keep the
    estimate current as your pattern shifts, whether that's a temporary
    change from travel or illness or a longer-term shift like the one
    perimenopause can bring. The <a href="/features" class="underline">full
    feature list</a> covers how LunaTrack builds predictions from logged
    cycles rather than a single manual entry, alongside the rest of what it
    tracks day to day.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you came here just to check a date, that's a completely reasonable
    use of this page on its own — not everyone wants an app for one
    calculation. But if the number kept moving because your actual period
    didn't land where the last calculation said it would, that's usually a
    sign that a single average has stopped capturing what your body is
    doing, and an ongoing log will tell you more than typing the same three
    numbers in again next month.
  </p>
</Tool>
